1. Fully Non-Blocking Netty EventLoops (Fix Thread Starvation)
Currently, in ClientHandler.java, when a client sends a PRODUCE_BATCH_REQUEST, the handler does this:

java
baseOffset = raftNode.proposeBatch(...); // This blocks!
Inside proposeBatch, it calls future.get(...), which completely blocks the Netty I/O thread until the Raft cluster reaches consensus. If you have 16 Netty threads, you can only handle 16 concurrent requests before the broker stalls. The Fix: Make proposeBatch return the CompletableFuture<Long>. In ClientHandler, use .thenAccept(offset -> ctx.writeAndFlush(...)) so the Netty thread is immediately freed up to process other clients while Raft does its work asynchronously.

2. Zero-Copy & Memory-Mapped Files (mmap)
Right now, RaftLog uses RandomAccessFile and explicitly allocates byte[] data = new byte[length] to read/write bytes. This requires copying bytes from the disk into the OS memory, then into the JVM heap. The Fix: Use Java's FileChannel.map() to create a MappedByteBuffer. This maps the file directly into virtual memory (off-heap). Reading and writing to it is as fast as writing to RAM, and you avoid Java heap allocations entirely.

3. Parse Protobufs directly from Off-Heap Memory
In ClientHandler, your pipeline resolves to a byte[]:

java
protected void channelRead0(ChannelHandlerContext ctx, byte[] msg) {
    MessageEnvelope envelope = MessageEnvelope.parseFrom(msg);
}
This forces Netty to copy data from the network socket (Direct Memory) into the Java Heap (byte[]) before Protobuf even starts parsing it. The Fix: Change the pipeline to pass Netty's native ByteBuf. You can wrap the ByteBuf's internal NIO buffer in a CodedInputStream and pass it to Protobuf, completely skipping the intermediate byte[] heap allocation.

4. Raft Group Commit (Fsync Batching)
In RaftLog.append(entry), you are calling raf.getFD().sync() for every single proposed entry. Even on an NVMe SSD, this hard-limits your throughput to the physical IOPS limit of the drive (e.g., 50,000 ops/sec). The Fix: Implement "Group Commit". Instead of flushing to disk on every single append, allow a dedicated background thread (or the Raft leader loop) to batch multiple pending entries and execute a single .sync() for all of them at once. This massively amortizes the expensive disk flush operations.








